---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Presence from '@/components/motion/scenes/Presence.astro';
import { PresenceControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/PresenceControls';
import { MotionPresenceReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-presence.tsx';
import { MotionPresenceAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-presence.ts';
import MotionPresenceSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-presence.svelte';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="presence"
  title="Motion: show and hide"
  description="Show and hide an element with an entry and an exit, in HTML, React, Angular and Svelte: the exit plays before the element is gone."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Set <code>hidden</code> on an element and its exit plays before it disappears. Remove <code>hidden</code>, or insert the element, and its entry plays.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Wrap an element in <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>: when you stop rendering it, its exit plays before React removes it, and when you render it, its entry plays.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion</code> on an element inside <code>&#64;if</code>: Angular keeps it until its exit has played, and plays its entry when it comes back.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion()</code> and <code>transition:presence</code> to an element inside <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code>: Svelte keeps it until its exit has played, and plays its entry when it comes back.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="presence" label="Live example: Show and hide" height={240}>
    <Fragment slot="controls">
      <PresenceControls client:visible toggleId="presence-page-toggle" cardId="presence-page-card" />
    </Fragment>
    <Presence id="presence-page-card" toggleId="presence-page-toggle" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={chipClass} data-presence-readout>data-motion="slide"</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Show and hide an element</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Put <code>data-motion</code> on the element with the name of a motion, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once for the page. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Set <code>hidden</code> to hide the element: its exit plays, then it is hidden.</li>
        <li>Remove <code>hidden</code> to show it again, or insert a new element: its entry plays.</li>
        <li>Insert an element with <code>hidden</code> when it must wait to be shown: it plays its entry when you remove the attribute.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with the name of a motion and a <code>key</code>, inside a <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>, and show it with a condition. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>When you stop rendering it, <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> keeps it on the page until its exit has played, then removes it.</li>
        <li>When you render it again, its entry plays.</li>
        <li>Give each child of <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> a <code>key</code>: that is how it knows which child left.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>Add <code>Motion</code> to your component's <code>imports</code>, put <code>udxMotion</code> on the element with the name of a motion, and show it with <code>&#64;if</code>. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>When the condition turns false, Angular keeps the element until its exit has played, then removes it.</li>
        <li>When the condition turns true again, its entry plays.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>Attach <code>motion()</code> to the element with the name of a motion, add <code>transition:presence</code>, and show it with <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code>. Then:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>When the condition turns false, <code>presence</code> keeps the element until its exit has played, then Svelte removes it.</li>
        <li>When the condition turns true again, its entry plays.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    The example uses the preset <code>slide</code>: the card rises 24 px as it fades in, and goes back down as it fades
    out.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence" previewHeight={272}>
    <MotionPresenceReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionPresenceAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionPresenceSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>
  <ul>
    <ForFramework as="li">
      <Fragment slot="html">An element that is on the page when you call <code>connectMotion()</code> plays no entry: only the ones you show or insert afterwards do.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="react">Content rendered by the server plays no entry when React hydrates it: only what you render afterwards does.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="angular">Content rendered by the server plays no entry when Angular hydrates it: only what you show afterwards does.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="svelte">Content rendered by the server plays its entry again when Svelte hydrates it. Pass <code>settled: true</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code> for an element that is already in place.</Fragment>
    </ForFramework>
    <li>While its exit plays, the element is <code>inert</code>: it can no longer be clicked or focused.</li>
    <li>
      If you show the element again during its exit, its entry starts from where the exit is: nothing jumps.
    </li>
    <li>
      The runtime writes <code>data-motion-presence</code> on the element: <code>entering</code>, <code>present</code> or
      <code>exiting</code>. Style these states with Tailwind, such as
      <code>data-[motion-presence=exiting]:pointer-events-none</code>, and do not set the attribute yourself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Give focus back when the element leaves</h2>
  <p>
    When the element leaves with focus inside it, such as a panel closed by its own Close button, the browser drops focus
    once the element turns <code>inert</code>. Name where focus goes, and the exit moves it there first (WCAG 2.4.3).
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>data-motion-restore-focus</code> to a CSS selector: it is looked up when the exit starts, and it must match an element outside the one that leaves.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>restoreFocus</code> to <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>: an element, or a function that returns one when the exit starts, such as the button that opened the panel.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>[udxMotionRestoreFocus]</code> to an element, such as a template reference to the button that opened the panel, or to a function that returns one when the exit starts.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>restoreFocus</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>: an element, or a function that returns one when the exit starts, such as the button that opened the panel.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence-focus" live={false} />
  <p>
    If you name no target, the element stays focusable until focus leaves it or the exit ends, and a development build
    warns you once.
  </p>

  <h2>Drive the presence with a boolean</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>
        Without <code>connectMotion()</code>, bind the element in JavaScript with <code>motion()</code>, and call
        <code>setPresent(false)</code> to play its exit and <code>setPresent(true)</code> to play its entry. Pass
        <code>settled: true</code> for an element that is already on the page, so that it plays no entry when you bind it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>setPresent()</code> resolves to <code>'finished'</code>, or to <code>'cancelled'</code> when a later call
        took over. It never rejects: if anything fails, the element is left in its end state. After its exit, the element
        stays in the page, transparent and <code>inert</code>, and keeps its space. To take it out of the layout as well,
        set <code>hidden</code> on it once <code>setPresent(false)</code> has resolved to <code>'finished'</code>.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>
        Use <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> to show and hide content that is transient, such as a toast or a dialog: it is
        removed once its exit has played. For navigation and content, such as a menu or an accordion answer, whose links and
        text must stay in the server HTML, keep the element in the DOM with <code>present</code>: <code>&lt;Motion
        present=&#123;open&#125;&gt;</code> plays the entry when <code>present</code> turns true, and its exit when it turns
        false, then <code>hidden</code> hides it. The element is rendered <code>hidden</code> from the server render on, so no
        <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> is needed.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <code>useMotion</code>, render the element always, with the <code>present</code> option, and set
        <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> on it: the same behaviour.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>
        To keep the element in the DOM, bind <code>[udxMotionPresent]</code> instead of using <code>&#64;if</code>: the
        exit plays when it turns false, and then <code>hidden</code> hides the element. The entry plays when it turns true,
        and <code>hidden</code> goes. Angular sets <code>hidden</code> from the first render, so the server HTML hides a closed
        element, and its content stays indexable. Transient UI keeps <code>&#64;if</code>.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>
        To keep the element in the DOM, pass <code>present</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code> instead of using
        <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code>: the exit plays when it turns false, and then <code>hidden</code> hides the element. The
        entry plays when it turns true, and <code>hidden</code> goes. The server runs no attachment, so write
        <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> on the element as well: that is what hides it in the server HTML.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence-flag" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>
    Define your own presence with <code>defineMotion()</code> and the verb <code>appear</code>, which enters through its
    keyframes and leaves backward, or <code>disappear</code>, which does the reverse. Both take these options. To make
    the elements that enter together follow one another, see <a href="/motion/lists">Lists</a>; to split a text into
    words, see <a href="/motion/text">Text</a>.
  </p>
  <MotionOptions verb="appear" />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>
    Without a <code>timing</code>, an entry moves on the default spatial spring and fades on the default effects spring,
    and an exit plays on the default effects spring.
  </p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion (<code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>, or <code>data-motion-policy="reduce"</code> on
    <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>), the entry and the exit become a short fade, and the element does not move. With
    <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, it appears and disappears at once. See
    <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a> to set either one.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <ForFramework as="li">
      <Fragment slot="html">If you remove the element from the DOM, it goes at once, with no exit: set <code>hidden</code> instead.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="react">If you stop rendering the <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> itself, its children go at once, with no exit: keep it rendered, and put the condition inside it.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="angular">If the element that leaves is in a child component's template, Angular does not wait for its exit when it removes the child: put <code>udxMotion</code> on the child's host element, in the parent's template.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="svelte">If a parent block removes the element, Svelte plays no transition: put <code>transition:presence</code> on the element that its own <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> or <code>&#123;#each&#125;</code> block adds and removes.</Fragment>
    </ForFramework>
    <ForFramework as="li">
      <Fragment slot="react">If a child of <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> holds no <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, it leaves at once: put the motion on the element that must play the exit.</Fragment>
    </ForFramework>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
